{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wn5hyZmX8Cb2gUufqrLu8W","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, here's what I found:\n\n## Review Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation criteria:\n\n1. **Originality** - Novel problems/methods and clear differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** - Technical soundness and well-supported claims\n3. **Clarity** - Clear writing with sufficient detail for reproducibility\n4. **Significance** - Important results likely to influence the field\n5. **Relevance** - Addresses robot learning problems of interest to CoRL audience\n6. **Limitations** - Honest acknowledgment of shortcomings (strongly encouraged)\n\n## Scoring Scale\n\n**The document does not provide a numerical rating scale or specific score definitions.** No scoring rubric (e.g., 1-5, 1-10) is mentioned in the guidelines provided.\n\n## Key Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize being \"constructive and concrete\" while avoiding common reviewer mistakes such as:\n- Making unsupported novelty claims\n- Requiring specific methods (datasets, theorems, real robots) as mandatory\n- Rejecting based solely on SOTA performance\n\nThe document references external resources from ICML and NeurIPS for additional reviewer training but does not reproduce their scoring scales here."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"a86695c4-d6f6-4afa-b2d0-a54736f0d933","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:02:55.808Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, here's what I found:\n\n## Review Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation criteria:\n\n1. **Originality** - Novel problems/methods and clear differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** - Technical soundness and well-supported claims\n3. **Clarity** - Clear writing with sufficient detail for reproducibility\n4. **Significance** - Important results likely to influence the field\n5. **Relevance** - Addresses robot learning problems of interest to CoRL audience\n6. **Limitations** - Honest acknowledgment of shortcomings (strongly encouraged)\n\n## Scoring Scale\n\n**The document does not provide a numerical rating scale or specific score definitions.** No scoring rubric (e.g., 1-5, 1-10) is mentioned in the guidelines provided.\n\n## Key Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize being \"constructive and concrete\" while avoiding common reviewer mistakes such as:\n- Making unsupported novelty claims\n- Requiring specific methods (datasets, theorems, real robots) as mandatory\n- Rejecting based solely on SOTA performance\n\nThe document references external resources from ICML and NeurIPS for additional reviewer training but does not reproduce their scoring scales here.","durationMs":4448,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GHBoWR1tr6jbGzTo1LbFdt","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Review Guidelines - Available Information\n\nBased on the provided web page content, I can confirm the following:\n\n## Available Information\n\n**Review Criteria:**\nThe document states that submissions will be evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Scope Requirements:**\nAll submissions must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through either:\n- Intent: explicitly addressing a learning question for physical robots, OR\n- Outcome: testing the proposed solution on physical robots\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\nReviewers should have expertise demonstrated by \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals\" like CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, and core ML venues like NeurIPS, ICML, and AIStats.\n\n## Information NOT Provided\n\nThe page content does **not include**:\n- A numerical rating scale (e.g., 1-10)\n- Specific score definitions or meanings\n- Review form dimensions or evaluation categories\n- Conference acceptance rate\n\nThe extracted content focuses on desk rejection criteria and reviewer qualifications rather than the detailed scoring rubric and evaluation framework you're seeking."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"73480cc7-f5f5-4dfc-9bc1-773c9c51f8e4","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:02:59.762Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":134162,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Review Guidelines - Available Information\n\nBased on the provided web page content, I can confirm the following:\n\n## Available Information\n\n**Review Criteria:**\nThe document states that submissions will be evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Scope Requirements:**\nAll submissions must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through either:\n- Intent: explicitly addressing a learning question for physical robots, OR\n- Outcome: testing the proposed solution on physical robots\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\nReviewers should have expertise demonstrated by \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals\" like CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, and core ML venues like NeurIPS, ICML, and AIStats.\n\n## Information NOT Provided\n\nThe page content does **not include**:\n- A numerical rating scale (e.g., 1-10)\n- Specific score definitions or meanings\n- Review form dimensions or evaluation categories\n- Conference acceptance rate\n\nThe extracted content focuses on desk rejection criteria and reviewer qualifications rather than the detailed scoring rubric and evaluation framework you're seeking.","durationMs":8922,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
