{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PwMx3nkioJWE53J7A6C6pS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012Mc96SY3Md8YkdvY6AzRkF","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/diff_collision_sdf/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T223036/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1084,"cache_read_input_tokens":44397,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1084,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5VeGVrkSVRPRdTHcwYQGv","session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"01205352-0cf0-4c41-8019-b4e6e327dfce"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Mc96SY3Md8YkdvY6AzRkF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{caption}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98\\% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion}\n15\t\n16\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n17\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t% ============================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models are increasingly deployed in safety-critical robotic manipulation, yet standard safety metrics may paint a misleadingly optimistic picture.\n27\tWe present a large-scale empirical study (70+ experiments, 6000+ episodes) revealing that the widely-used Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) metric is \\emph{systematically contaminated} by two failure modes: \\textbf{action collapse} (the robot freezes, trivially achieving ``collision-free'' status) and \\textbf{geometric path safety} (conservative motions that happen to avoid obstacles without intentional avoidance behavior).\n28\tAcross three model families---Pi0 (flow matching, 170M), Pi0.5 (flow matching, 300M), and OpenVLA (autoregressive, 7B)---we find that 92--99.6\\% of episodes classified as ``safe'' by CAR exhibit one of these fake safety modes.\n29\tWe propose \\emph{displacement-filtered CAR}, a corrected metric that distinguishes genuine avoidance from inaction.\n30\tAs a constructive contribution, we show that a Signed Distance Field (SDF) auxiliary training loss reliably induces \\emph{real} collision avoidance (+25 percentage points filtered CAR, 0\\% fake safety) on a capable VLA (Pi0.5), though at severe task performance cost (TSR: 73\\%$\\to$7\\%).\n31\tWe further demonstrate a two-phase training strategy---SDF pre-training followed by standard fine-tuning---that recovers task performance to 48\\% TSR while retaining 81\\% of the acquired safety encoding.\n32\tOur findings call for fundamental revision of VLA safety evaluation methodology and provide a concrete path toward models that are both capable and genuinely safe.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\\label{sec:intro}\n38\t\n39\tThe deployment of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models in real-world robotic manipulation demands reliable safety guarantees. The Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)---defined as the fraction of episodes in which the robot does not contact designated obstacles---has become the de facto safety metric in benchmarks such as SafeLIBERO. High CAR scores are routinely cited as evidence that models have learned meaningful collision avoidance behavior.\n40\t\n41\tWe challenge this assumption. Through systematic evaluation across three architecturally distinct VLA models, two manipulation scenes, and thousands of episodes, we uncover a fundamental flaw: \\textbf{the CAR metric cannot distinguish genuine collision avoidance from mere inaction}. A model that freezes in place (action collapse) or one that moves conservatively without spatial awareness of obstacles (geometric path safety) both achieve high CAR---yet neither has learned to avoid collisions in any meaningful sense.\n42\t\n43\tOur contributions are:\n44\t\\begin{enumerate}\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Fake safety taxonomy}: We identify and quantify two systematic contamination modes of CAR---action collapse and geometric path safety---showing they account for 92--99.6\\% of nominally ``safe'' episodes across three model families (Section~\\ref{sec:fake_safety}).\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Displacement-filtered CAR}: We propose a corrected metric that filters out episodes with end-effector displacement below a physically meaningful threshold, yielding ``real CAR'' that measures genuine avoidance behavior (Section~\\ref{sec:metric}).\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{SDF auxiliary loss as diagnostic probe}: We demonstrate that training-time SDF supervision produces verifiable real collision avoidance (+25pp filtered CAR), establishing a ground-truth reference for metric validation (Section~\\ref{sec:sdf_training}).\n48\t    \\item \\textbf{Two-phase safety encoding}: We show that SDF-acquired safety representations persist after removing the auxiliary loss, enabling recovery of task performance (TSR: 9\\%$\\to$48\\%) while retaining 81\\% of safety encoding (Section~\\ref{sec:two_phase}).\n49\t\\end{enumerate}\n50\t\n51\t% ============================================================\n52\t\\section{Method}\n53\t\\label{sec:method}\n54\t\n55\t\\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\n56\t\\label{sec:metric}\n57\t\n58\tStandard CAR classifies episode $i$ as safe if maximum obstacle displacement $d_i^{\\text{obs}} < \\epsilon$ (typically $\\epsilon = 1$mm). We observe that this criterion is trivially satisfied when the robot barely moves. We define \\emph{displacement-filtered CAR} (dfCAR):\n59\t\\begin{equation}\n60\t    \\text{dfCAR} = \\frac{|\\{i : d_i^{\\text{obs}} < \\epsilon \\;\\wedge\\; d_i^{\\text{ee}} > \\delta\\}|}{N}\n61\t\\end{equation}\n62\twhere $d_i^{\\text{ee}}$ is the maximum end-effector displacement in episode $i$ and $\\delta$ is a motion threshold (we use $\\delta = 0.1$m based on the minimum displacement needed to complete any SafeLIBERO task). Episodes with $d_i^{\\text{ee}} \\leq \\delta$ that are classified as safe by standard CAR are labeled \\emph{fake safe}.\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Training Loss}\n65\t\\label{sec:sdf_loss}\n66\t\n67\tTo produce verifiably real collision avoidance behavior, we augment the standard action prediction loss with a Signed Distance Field (SDF) auxiliary objective. Given end-effector position $\\mathbf{p}_t$ and the SDF of the scene $\\phi(\\cdot)$, we define:\n68\t\\begin{equation}\n69\t    \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SDF}} = \\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\max(0, \\; \\tau - \\phi(\\mathbf{p}_t))^2\n70\t\\end{equation}\n71\twhere $\\tau$ is a safety margin. The total training loss is:\n72\t\\begin{equation}\n73\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}} + \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SDF}}\n74\t\\end{equation}\n75\tWe apply this loss through LoRA adapters on Pi0.5, with $\\lambda \\in \\{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$.\n76\t\n77\t\\subsection{Two-Phase Training Strategy}\n78\t\\label{sec:two_phase_method}\n79\t\n80\tThe SDF loss produces real avoidance but devastates task performance. We hypothesize that safety-relevant representations can be \\emph{encoded} during a short SDF phase and \\emph{retained} during subsequent standard training. Our two-phase protocol:\n81\t\\begin{itemize}\n82\t    \\item \\textbf{Phase 1} (steps 0--10K): Train with $\\lambda = 0.005$ to establish safety encoding in LoRA weights.\n83\t    \\item \\textbf{Phase 2} (steps 10K--30K): Resume training with $\\lambda = 0$, allowing task performance to recover while preserving internalized safety representations.\n84\t\\end{itemize}\n85\t\n86\t% ============================================================\n87\t\\section{Experiments}\n88\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n89\t\n90\tWe evaluate on SafeLIBERO, using two scenes (\\texttt{object\\_pudding} with 4 manipulation tasks and \\texttt{spatial\\_bowl} with 4 spatial reasoning tasks). All evaluations use 200 episodes unless noted otherwise.\n91\t\n92\t\\subsection{Fake Safety is Pervasive Across Architectures}\n93\t\\label{sec:fake_safety}\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Action collapse in Pi0+LoRA+SDF (A1).}\n96\tOur initial SDF-trained model (A1: Pi0 + LoRA + SDF) achieves CAR = 91\\% but TSR = 0\\%. Analysis of 800 episodes reveals median obstacle displacement of $\\sim$5nm and 99.6\\% of safe episodes showing obstacle displacement $< 0.1$mm. We validate with end-effector displacement on 50 episodes (Table~\\ref{tab:a1_ee}): 96\\% of safe episodes show EE displacement $< 1$mm, confirming the robot is frozen rather than avoiding.\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{table}[h]\n99\t\\centering\n100\t\\caption{A1 (Pi0+LoRA+SDF) displacement analysis confirming action collapse. Two independent metrics---obstacle displacement (800 episodes) and end-effector displacement (50 episodes)---converge on the same conclusion.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5VeGVrkSVRPRdTHcwYQGv","session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"9ea3d695-482a-4b12-9949-4a0b40e664f5","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:05:29.503Z"}
