{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FYqcPicCWXoE8sA5WnvKkb","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t\\caption{A1 (Pi0+LoRA+SDF) displacement analysis confirming action collapse. Two independent metrics---obstacle displacement (800 episodes) and end-effector displacement (50 episodes)---converge on the same conclusion.}\n101\t\\label{tab:a1_ee}\n102\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n103\t\\toprule\n104\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Obstacle Disp (800ep)} & \\textbf{EE Disp (50ep)} \\\\\n105\t\\midrule\n106\tAvg safe episode displacement & 0.002mm & 1.8mm \\\\\n107\tMedian safe episode displacement & $\\sim$5nm & 0.5mm \\\\\n108\t\\% safe episodes $<$ 0.1mm & 99.6\\% & 96\\% \\\\\n109\tFake safe rate & 99.6\\% & 98\\% \\\\\n110\tTSR & 0\\% & 0\\% \\\\\n111\tCAR & 91.5\\% & 91\\% \\\\\n112\t\\bottomrule\n113\t\\end{tabular}\n114\t\\end{table}\n115\t\n116\t\\paragraph{Cross-architecture validation with OpenVLA.}\n117\tTo test whether fake safety is architecture-specific, we evaluate OpenVLA-7B (LLaVA-based autoregressive VLA) zero-shot on SafeLIBERO (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_model}). OpenVLA exhibits \\emph{both} failure modes: 55\\% of episodes show action collapse (OOD action decoder degeneration) and 37\\% show geometric path safety (conservative short-range motions). Only 3\\% of safe episodes display genuine avoidance behavior. With CAR = 88\\% but fake safe rate = 92\\%, OpenVLA confirms that fake safety is not specific to flow-matching architectures or SDF training---it is a systematic bias of CAR toward low-activity episodes.\n118\t\n119\t\\begin{table}[h]\n120\t\\centering\n121\t\\caption{Cross-model fake safety comparison. Three model families spanning two architectural paradigms all exhibit $>$90\\% fake safety rates under standard CAR.}\n122\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n123\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n124\t\\toprule\n125\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Architecture} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{Fake Safe\\%} & \\textbf{Mechanism} \\\\\n126\t\\midrule\n127\tPi0 (A1) & Flow Matching & 170M & 0 & 91.5 & 99.6 & Action collapse \\\\\n128\tPi0.5 (baseline) & Flow Matching & 300M & 74.7 & 26.8 & 98.2$^*$ & Geometric path \\\\\n129\tOpenVLA-7B & AR Token & 7B & 5 & 88 & 92 & Mixed \\\\\n130\t\\bottomrule\n131\t\\end{tabular}\n132\t\\vspace{2pt}\n133\t{\\small $^*$Pi0.5 fake safety computed over safe episodes' displacement distribution (56/200 episodes).}\n134\t\\end{table}\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA per-task analysis.}\n137\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla_tasks} shows per-task breakdown revealing task-dependent dominant fake safety modes. Tasks with near-zero TSR (orange\\_juice, milk) exhibit primarily action collapse, while chocolate\\_pudding (highest TSR at 14\\%) shows geometric path dominance.\n138\t\n139\t\\begin{table}[h]\n140\t\\centering\n141\t\\caption{OpenVLA-7B per-task results on \\texttt{object\\_pudding} (200 episodes total).}\n142\t\\label{tab:openvla_tasks}\n143\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n144\t\\toprule\n145\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{Obs. Disp (mm)} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} & \\textbf{Dominant Mode} \\\\\n146\t\\midrule\n147\torange\\_juice & 2 & 94 & 1.2 & 0.032 & Action collapse \\\\\n148\tchocolate\\_pudding & 14 & 76 & 18.5 & 0.112 & Geometric path \\\\\n149\tmilk & 4 & 96 & 0.8 & 0.028 & Action collapse \\\\\n150\tbbq\\_sauce & 0 & 86 & 3.1 & 0.088 & Mixed \\\\\n151\t\\midrule\n152\tAverage & 5 & 88 & 5.9 & 0.065 & --- \\\\\n153\t\\bottomrule\n154\t\\end{tabular}\n155\t\\end{table}\n156\t\n157\t\\subsection{SDF Attribution: Geometric Supervision is the Driver}\n158\t\\label{sec:sdf_training}\n159\t\n160\tA critical question for interpreting SDF's safety improvement is whether the LoRA fine-tuning procedure itself (without SDF) might improve CAR. We train Pi0.5 + LoRA without SDF loss (2 seeds, 30K steps) and find CAR = 27$\\pm$1.4\\%---statistically identical to the zero-shot baseline (26.8$\\pm$1.6\\%). This establishes that the \\textbf{entire +25pp CAR improvement} under SDF training is attributable to geometric SDF supervision, not to LoRA adaptation.\n161\t\n162\t\\begin{table}[h]\n163\t\\centering\n164\t\\caption{LoRA attribution analysis. LoRA-only training does not affect collision avoidance; the full CAR delta is attributable to SDF auxiliary loss.}\n165\t\\label{tab:lora_attribution}\n166\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n167\t\\toprule\n168\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$CAR vs LoRA} \\\\\n169\t\\midrule\n170\tPi0.5 zero-shot (3-seed) & 74.7$\\pm$1.5 & 26.8$\\pm$1.6 & 0.151 & --- \\\\\n171\tPi0.5 + LoRA (2-seed) & 71$\\pm$1.4 & 27$\\pm$1.4 & 0.152 & --- \\\\\n172\tPi0.5 + LoRA + SDF $\\lambda$=0.005 (3-seed) & 6.8$\\pm$1.8 & 52$\\pm$2.1 & 0.498 & +25pp \\\\\n173\tPi0.5 + LoRA + SDF $\\lambda$=0.01 (3-seed) & 6.8$\\pm$1.0 & 52.8$\\pm$0.3 & $\\sim$0.50 & +25.8pp \\\\\n174\t\\bottomrule\n175\t\\end{tabular}\n176\t\\end{table}\n177\t\n178\t\\paragraph{Per-task variation.} Table~\\ref{tab:per_task_lora} reveals interesting asymmetry: task \\texttt{milk} shows +70pp CAR improvement from SDF while \\texttt{bbq\\_sauce} shows $-$24pp, likely related to obstacle geometry---consistent with the EE-only SDF limitation (the SDF gradient pushes the end-effector away but may cause the arm body to sweep through obstacles positioned at different heights).\n179\t\n180\t\\begin{table}[h]\n181\t\\centering\n182\t\\caption{Per-task LoRA vs SDF comparison (seed 0, $\\lambda$=0.005) on \\texttt{object\\_pudding}.}\n183\t\\label{tab:per_task_lora}\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{LoRA TSR\\%} & \\textbf{LoRA CAR\\%} & \\textbf{SDF TSR\\%} & \\textbf{SDF CAR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR $\\Delta$} \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\torange\\_juice & 60 & 8 & 2 & 58 & +50pp \\\\\n189\tchocolate\\_pudding & 82 & 50 & 20 & 42 & $-$8pp \\\\\n190\tmilk & 68 & 16 & 0 & 86 & +70pp \\\\\n191\tbbq\\_sauce & 70 & 38 & 0 & 14 & $-$24pp \\\\\n192\t\\bottomrule\n193\t\\end{tabular}\n194\t\\end{table}\n195\t\n196\t\\subsection{Dose-Dependent Safety: The Lambda Curve}\n197\t\\label{sec:lambda}\n198\t\n199\tWe construct a 4-point $\\lambda$ curve to characterize the dose-response relationship between SDF loss weight and safety/performance tradeoff (Table~\\ref{tab:lambda_curve}).\n200\t\n201\t\\begin{table}[h]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\caption{Four-point $\\lambda$ curve showing dose-dependent safety-performance tradeoff. CAR increases monotonically with $\\lambda$ but with diminishing returns; $\\lambda$=0.005 captures 69\\% of maximum CAR improvement.}\n204\t\\label{tab:lambda_curve}\n205\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n206\t\\toprule\n207\t$\\boldsymbol{\\lambda}$ & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} & \\textbf{Fake Safe\\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$CAR vs LoRA} \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\t0 (LoRA-only) & 71 & 27 & 0.15 & 98 & --- \\\\\n210\t0.005 & 6.8 & 52 & 0.50 & 0 & +25pp \\\\\n211\t0.01 & 6.8 & 53 & 0.50 & 0 & +26pp \\\\\n212\t0.1 & 1.5 & 63 & 0.38 & 8 & +36pp \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\tThree observations emerge: (1) the effect is dose-dependent---CAR increases monotonically with $\\lambda$; (2) diminishing returns---$\\lambda=0.005$ already captures the majority of safety gain (+25 of +36pp maximum); (3) excessive $\\lambda$ introduces a new failure mode---at $\\lambda=0.1$, EE displacement \\emph{decreases} to 0.38m and fake safe rate rises to 8\\%, suggesting overly conservative motions approaching action collapse. The optimal operating point lies in the $\\lambda \\in [0.005, 0.01]$ range.\n218\t\n219\t\\subsection{Two-Phase Safety Encoding}\n220\t\\label{sec:two_phase}\n221\t\n222\tWe test whether safety representations established during SDF training persist after removing the auxiliary loss. Starting from a Phase 1 checkpoint (10K steps, $\\lambda=0.005$), we continue training for 20K additional steps with $\\lambda=0$ (Phase 2).\n223\t\n224\t\\begin{table}[h]\n225\t\\centering\n226\t\\caption{Two-phase training results. Phase 2 ($\\lambda$=0 resume) recovers task performance while retaining majority of safety encoding. Simultaneous TSR$\\geq$40\\% and CAR$\\geq$40\\% confirms the viability of two-phase safety encoding.}\n227\t\\label{tab:phase2}\n228\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n229\t\\toprule\n230\t\\textbf{Phase} & $\\boldsymbol{\\lambda}$ & \\textbf{Steps} & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} & \\textbf{Fake Safe\\%} \\\\\n231\t\\midrule\n232\tBaseline (LoRA-only) & --- & 30K & 71 & 27 & 0.15 & 98 \\\\\n233\tPhase 1 only (SDF) & 0.005 & 10K & 9 & 55.5 & 0.50 & 0 \\\\\n234\tPhase 1 only (SDF) & 0.005 & 30K & 6.8 & 52 & 0.50 & 0 \\\\\n235\t\\textbf{Phase 2 (SDF$\\to\\lambda$=0)} & \\textbf{0.005$\\to$0} & \\textbf{10K+20K} & \\textbf{48} & \\textbf{45} & \\textbf{0.42} & \\textbf{5} \\\\\n236\tPi0.5 zero-shot & --- & --- & 74.7 & 26.8 & 0.15 & 98 \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\tPhase 2 achieves TSR = 48\\% and CAR = 45\\% simultaneously, with only 5\\% fake safety and EE displacement of 0.42m confirming active robot motion. Compared to Phase 1 (TSR=9\\%, CAR=55.5\\%), Phase 2 recovers +39pp task success while retaining 81\\% of safety encoding (45/55.5). Compared to baseline (TSR=71\\%, CAR=27\\%), Phase 2 trades 23pp TSR for +18pp \\emph{genuine} CAR---a meaningful and actionable tradeoff.\n242\t\n243\t\\paragraph{Per-task retention analysis.} Table~\\ref{tab:phase2_tasks} shows that safety retention is consistent across tasks (76--100\\%), with the highest absolute CAR in tasks where SDF training was most effective (milk: 72\\% CAR retained from Phase 1's 92\\%).\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[h]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\caption{Phase 2 per-task breakdown showing consistent TSR recovery and CAR retention.}\n248\t\\label{tab:phase2_tasks}\n249\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n250\t\\toprule\n251\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Ph1 TSR\\%} & \\textbf{Ph2 TSR\\%} & \\textbf{Recovery} & \\textbf{Ph1 CAR\\%} & \\textbf{Ph2 CAR\\%} & \\textbf{Retention} \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\torange\\_juice & 0 & 38 & +38pp & 68 & 52 & 76\\% \\\\\n254\tchocolate\\_pudding & 36 & 68 & +32pp & 44 & 38 & 86\\% \\\\\n255\tmilk & 0 & 42 & +42pp & 92 & 72 & 78\\% \\\\\n256\tbbq\\_sauce & 0 & 44 & +44pp & 18 & 18 & 100\\% \\\\\n257\t\\midrule\n258\t\\textbf{Average} & 9 & 48 & +39pp & 55.5 & 45 & 81\\% \\\\\n259\t\\bottomrule\n260\t\\end{tabular}\n261\t\\end{table}\n262\t\n263\t\\subsection{Cross-Scene Generalization}\n264\t\\label{sec:cross_scene}\n265\t\n266\tTo verify that SDF's effect is not scene-specific, we evaluate on \\texttt{spatial\\_bowl}---a geometrically distinct scene with distributed obstacles and higher arm-body collision risk (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_scene}).\n267\t\n268\t\\begin{table}[h]\n269\t\\centering\n270\t\\caption{Cross-scene SDF effect. Both scenes show consistent CAR improvement, TSR reduction, and elimination of fake safety.}\n271\t\\label{tab:cross_scene}\n272\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n273\t\\toprule\n274\t\\textbf{Scene} & \\textbf{BL TSR\\%} & \\textbf{SDF TSR\\%} & \\textbf{BL CAR\\%} & \\textbf{SDF CAR\\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$CAR} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\tobject\\_pudding (3-seed) & 74.7 & 6.8 & 26.8 & 52 & +25.2pp & 0.50 \\\\\n277\tspatial\\_bowl (seed 0) & 57.5 & 12 & 13 & 45 & +32pp & 0.43 \\\\\n278\t\\bottomrule\n279\t\\end{tabular}\n280\t\\end{table}\n281\t\n282\tThe SDF effect generalizes across scenes: both show substantial CAR improvement (+25--32pp), severe TSR degradation, EE displacement $>$0.4m (real avoidance), and $\\leq$2\\% fake safety. The slightly larger $\\Delta$CAR in \\texttt{spatial\\_bowl} (+32pp vs +25pp) may reflect more uniformly distributed obstacles providing more consistent SDF gradient directions.\n283\t\n284\t\\paragraph{spatial\\_bowl per-task results.} Table~\\ref{tab:spatial_tasks} shows relatively uniform SDF effect across spatial tasks (CAR 40--52\\%), contrasting with the high per-task variance in \\texttt{object\\_pudding} (14--86\\%). This supports the interpretation that geometrically uniform obstacle distributions yield more consistent SDF benefit.\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[h]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{spatial\\_bowl per-task results under SDF training ($\\lambda$=0.005).}\n289\t\\label{tab:spatial_tasks}\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{TSR\\%} & \\textbf{CAR\\%} & \\textbf{EE Disp (m)} \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\tpick\\_bowl\\_between\\_plate\\_ramekin & 8 & 52 & 0.46 \\\\\n295\tpick\\_bowl\\_on\\_ramekin & 6 & 40 & 0.38 \\\\\n296\tpick\\_bowl\\_on\\_stove & 18 & 48 & 0.44 \\\\\n297\tpick\\_bowl\\_on\\_cabinet & 16 & 40 & 0.42 \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tAverage & 12 & 45 & 0.43 \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t% ============================================================\n305\t\\section{Discussion}\n306\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n307\t\n308\t\\paragraph{Implications for VLA safety benchmarking.}\n309\tOur findings reveal that CAR---as currently computed---is not a reliable indicator of learned collision avoidance. Any model with low task capability (whether due to out-of-distribution inputs, insufficient training, or architectural limitations) will achieve high CAR through inaction rather than avoidance. We recommend that future VLA safety evaluations report displacement-filtered CAR alongside standard CAR, and treat high CAR with low TSR as a red flag rather than a positive signal.\n310\t\n311\t\\paragraph{The EE-only limitation.}\n312\tWhile SDF auxiliary loss successfully induces end-effector avoidance, per-task analysis reveals structural limitations: tasks where obstacles are positioned at arm-link height show reduced or negative CAR improvement (e.g., bbq\\_sauce: $-$24pp). Full-body SDF supervision---requiring collision geometry for all robot links---is a natural but computationally expensive extension.\n313\t\n314\t\\paragraph{Two-phase training as practical path.}\n315\tThe two-phase strategy (short SDF pre-training + standard fine-tuning) offers a practical middle ground: 81\\% safety retention with substantial task performance recovery. This suggests that VLA representations can \\emph{internalize} geometric safety constraints without requiring continuous explicit supervision---analogous to curriculum learning where early-stage constraints shape the learned representation space.\n316\t\n317\t% ============================================================\n318\t\\section{Conclusion}\n319\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n320\t\n321\tWe have demonstrated that standard collision avoidance metrics for VLA models are fundamentally misleading: across three model families and two manipulation scenes, 92--99.6\\% of episodes classified as ``safe'' exhibit fake safety through action collapse or geometric path coincidence. Our displacement-filtered CAR provides a corrected evaluation tool, and SDF auxiliary training establishes both the existence of the problem (by producing verifiably real avoidance as contrast) and a partial solution path via two-phase training. The safety-performance tradeoff remains challenging (TSR 48\\% vs baseline 73\\% at CAR 45\\%), but the demonstration that safety representations can be durably encoded opens promising directions for VLA safety training.\n322\t\n323\t% ============================================================\n324\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n325\t\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n326\t\n327\t\\bibitem{safelibero}\n328\tSafeLIBERO Benchmark, 2025.\n329\tSafety evaluation suite for vision-language-action models in manipulation.\n330\t\n331\t\\bibitem{pi0}\n332\tPhysical Intelligence.\n333\tPi0: A vision-language-action flow matching model for general robot control.\n334\t\\textit{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n335\t\n336\t\\bibitem{openvla}\n337\tKim et al.\n338\tOpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n339\t\\textit{ICML}, 2024.\n340\t\n341\t\\bibitem{sdf_robotics}\n342\tRatliff et al.\n343\tCHOMP: Gradient optimization techniques for efficient motion planning.\n344\t\\textit{ICRA}, 2009.\n345\t\n346\t\\bibitem{lora}\n347\tHu et al.\n348\tLoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n349\t\\textit{ICLR}, 2022.\n350\t\n351\t\\end{thebibliography}\n352\t\n353\t\\end{document}\n354\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5VeGVrkSVRPRdTHcwYQGv","session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"b32dfd18-837d-46c2-84cc-7fdb84d42b4e","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:05:33.863Z"}
