{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5VeGVrkSVRPRdTHcwYQGv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper across both the working drafts and the \"best-case\" dream version. Let me write the R2 review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper identifies systematic contamination of the Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) metric in VLA safety evaluation, showing that 92--99.6% of \"safe\" episodes across three model families (Pi0, Pi0.5, OpenVLA) are attributable to action collapse or geometric path safety rather than genuine obstacle avoidance. The authors propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) as a corrective metric, demonstrate that SDF auxiliary training loss produces real collision avoidance on Pi0.5 (+25pp filtered CAR, 0% fake safety) at severe task performance cost, and introduce a two-phase training strategy that partially recovers the safety-performance tradeoff (TSR 48%, CAR 45%). The paper positions itself as a diagnostic/evaluation methodology contribution with a constructive method component.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Compelling empirical observation with practical consequences.** The core finding that 98-99.6% of safe episodes are fake is well-quantified across 6000+ episodes and multiple model families. This is not merely an academic point: if downstream researchers compare collision avoidance methods using contaminated CAR, they will draw incorrect conclusions. The paper provides a clear mechanism (action collapse inflates displacement-based metrics) and a concrete fix (dfCAR). This has immediate practical value for the VLA safety community.\n\n- **Thorough experimental methodology for the diagnostic claims.** The paper uses multiple independent validation signals (obstacle displacement, EE displacement, TSR cross-check) to establish fake safety. The oracle experiments (ground-truth SDF with zero prediction error) convincingly isolate the structural limitation of EE-only refinement from SDF prediction quality. The 3-seed, 2-lambda experimental design for the SDF training claim is appropriate.\n\n- **The two-phase training result is genuinely interesting.** Demonstrating that safety representations encoded during SDF pre-training persist after removing the auxiliary loss (81% retention) is a non-trivial finding. The simultaneous TSR >= 40% and CAR >= 45% places the model in a previously unoccupied region of the TSR-CAR plane. This elevates the paper from pure diagnosis to a partial solution.\n\n- **Cross-architecture validation with OpenVLA.** Extending the fake safety finding beyond the Pi0 family to a 7B autoregressive VLA strengthens the generalizability claim. The identification of two distinct fake safety mechanisms (action collapse vs. geometric path safety) in OpenVLA adds taxonomic value.\n\n- **The per-task collision transfer analysis (bbq_sauce/bilateral corridor) is insightful.** This reveals a non-obvious limitation of point-wise SDF gradients in constrained geometries, which is useful for future method design.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"fake safety\" observation, while well-quantified, is not deeply novel.** The idea that a metric conflates inaction with safety is conceptually straightforward once stated. Action collapse is acknowledged as well-documented. The paper's own related work section cites a VLA safety survey (Li et al. 2026) that already identifies \"freezing attacks\" with \"the same behavioral signature.\" The contribution is quantification on a specific benchmark, not discovery of a new phenomenon. This is legitimate scientific work, but the novelty bar for a CoRL oral/spotlight is high.\n\n- **dfCAR is a minimal methodological contribution.** Adding a displacement threshold to filter out sub-threshold episodes is the obvious fix once the problem is identified. The paper does not develop theory around threshold selection (the 0.1mm threshold is empirically chosen, and the sensitivity analysis over 4 orders of magnitude mainly shows the problem is binary rather than continuous). There is no formal connection to construct validity frameworks or measurement theory that would make dfCAR a standalone methodological advance.\n\n- **SDF auxiliary loss for VLA training is an incremental combination of known techniques.** SDF-based collision constraints are well-established in motion planning (CHOMP, DiffCo, RAMP, etc.). LoRA fine-tuning is standard. The novelty is applying SDF as an auxiliary training loss to a VLA, but this is a straightforward adaptation. The paper does not introduce new SDF formulations, new loss designs, or new architectural components. The hinge loss on SDF distance (Eq. 2 in the dream version) is standard.\n\n- **The two-phase training strategy, while effective, is not positioned relative to extensive prior work on curriculum learning and progressive training.** Pre-training with an auxiliary objective then removing it is a well-known technique (e.g., auxiliary task pre-training, scheduled auxiliary losses in multi-task learning). The paper does not cite or discuss this connection, making the contribution appear more novel than it is.\n\n- **The safety-performance tradeoff remains severe and under-analyzed.** Even the best two-phase result (TSR 48%, CAR 45%) represents a 26pp TSR degradation from baseline (74.7%). The paper acknowledges this but does not provide sufficient analysis of *why* SDF loss degrades task performance so severely. Is it because the SDF gradient conflicts with task-relevant trajectories? Is it a representation interference issue? Without mechanistic understanding, the two-phase result is an empirical observation rather than a principled solution.\n\n- **The paper's scope is narrow relative to its claims.** The diagnostic claims are tested on SafeLIBERO (2 scenes from 1 benchmark) with Pi0-family models primarily. While OpenVLA adds breadth, the paper does not test on other manipulation benchmarks (RoboCasa, ManiSkill, RLBench) or non-tabletop settings. The claim that \"displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation\" is broader than the evidence supports.\n\n### Missing Related Work (specific papers/areas)\n\nThe paper's reference situation has improved significantly from the initial 5-reference draft to ~30 references in the working version. However, several important areas remain under-cited or absent:\n\n1. **Safe RL / Constrained RL foundations.** The paper cites SafeVLA but does not cite foundational constrained RL work (CPO, Achiam et al. 2017; Lagrangian methods for CMDPs; safety critics). The SDF auxiliary loss is implicitly a soft safety constraint, and the connection to constrained optimization should be made explicit.\n\n2. **Curriculum learning / progressive training.** The two-phase strategy is related to: scheduled auxiliary losses (Liebel & Korner, 2018), curriculum learning (Bengio et al., 2009), and progressive training in generative models. None are cited. This omission makes Contribution 4 appear more novel than it is.\n\n3. **Metric gaming / Goodhart's Law in robotics specifically.** The related work cites Goodhart's Law in RL (Karwowski et al. 2024) and annotation artifacts in NLP (Gururangan et al. 2018), but does not discuss metric gaming in robotics evaluation specifically. The success rate inflation problem in sim-to-real transfer and the reward hacking literature in robot learning are relevant.\n\n4. **Full-body collision detection in learning-based manipulation.** The paper identifies EE-only SDF as structurally limited but does not cite C-space distance fields (Li et al., RSS 2024), full-body SDF decomposition (Zhu et al. 2024), or whole-body manipulation with distance fields (Liu et al. 2023) -- all of which are in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper text. These are directly relevant to the EE-only limitation discussion.\n\n5. **3D-aware VLA methods beyond the collision-specific ones.** SpatialVLA, Spatial Forcing, DepthVLA, QDepth-VLA, and other spatial representation methods for VLAs are relevant context for understanding why SDF supervision helps -- it provides geometric grounding. Several are in the .bib file but only Spatial Forcing and QDepth-VLA appear to be cited in the related work.\n\n6. **Diffusion policy mode collapse.** Chi et al. (2023) is cited but the connection to the \"loss collapse\" phenomenon identified by Dong et al. (2025, \"Conditioning Matters\") is directly relevant to explaining why action collapse occurs under SDF training at high lambda values. This paper is in the literature map but not cited.\n\n7. **RAIL (Jung et al., ICRA 2025) and TamedPUMA (Bakker et al., L4DC 2025).** Both are test-time safety methods for imitation learning policies and are directly comparable to the oracle refinement approach tested in Section 4.3.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The fake safety finding relies heavily on SafeLIBERO's CAR definition. Have you verified that the original SafeLIBERO paper (VLSA/AEGIS) does not already discuss or account for action collapse when reporting CAR? If AEGIS achieves 77.85% CAR with non-zero TSR, the fake safety problem may be model-specific (Pi0 collapse) rather than metric-specific.\n\n2. For the two-phase training result: what happens if you run Phase 1 for fewer steps (e.g., 2K or 5K instead of 10K)? The 10K/20K split appears arbitrary. Is there a principled way to determine when Phase 1 has \"sufficiently encoded\" safety?\n\n3. The LoRA attribution experiment shows LoRA-only CAR = 27% (identical to zero-shot baseline), attributing the full +25pp to SDF. But LoRA-only also shows TSR = 71% vs. baseline 74.7%. Does the small TSR drop from LoRA fine-tuning interact with CAR in ways that could confound the attribution?\n\n4. At lambda = 0.1, fake safe rate rises to 8% and EE displacement drops. Is this a form of action collapse induced by the SDF loss itself (the robot learns to freeze near obstacles rather than navigate around them)? If so, there is a non-monotonic relationship between SDF loss weight and genuine safety that deserves more analysis.\n\n5. The paper tests on object_pudding (4 tasks) and spatial_bowl (4 tasks). SafeLIBERO has 32 scenarios across multiple suites. What prevented evaluation on the remaining 24 scenarios? The subset selection could introduce selection bias.\n\n6. For the oracle experiments: the oracle uses GT SDF but still operates on an action-collapsed base model (Pi0 with TSR ~0%). Have you tested oracle refinement on Pi0.5 (which actually moves)? The oracle failure could be primarily due to the base model's collapse rather than a structural EE-only limitation.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- **Originality: 5/10.** The core observation (action collapse inflates safety metrics) is conceptually simple and partially anticipated by prior work. dfCAR is a straightforward threshold filter. SDF auxiliary loss combines known components. The two-phase training is the most original element but is under-positioned relative to curriculum learning. The paper's value lies in rigorous quantification rather than conceptual novelty.\n\n- **Clarity: 7/10.** The paper is well-written with clear experimental methodology. The narrative arc (diagnosis -> structural analysis -> partial solution) is logical. The many placeholder values in the working draft and the still-pending experiments (LoRA baseline, Phase 2, OpenVLA) create uncertainty about the final paper's completeness. The dream version is more polished but contains numbers that may not all be verified.\n\n- **Significance: 6/10.** The fake safety finding is important for the VLA safety community and could change evaluation practices. However, the fix is simple (threshold filtering), the method contribution is preliminary (severe tradeoff remains), and the scope is limited to one benchmark family. The two-phase result, if confirmed with multi-seed experiments, would raise significance to 7.\n\n- **Relevance: 8/10.** Highly relevant to CoRL. VLA safety is a timely topic, SafeLIBERO is a recent and actively-used benchmark, and the paper addresses a problem that directly affects how the community evaluates collision avoidance methods. The robotics-specific analysis (EE-only limitation, arm-body collisions, workspace geometry effects) is appropriate for the venue.\n\n- **Overall: 5/10.** Marginally below acceptance threshold. The paper makes a valid and useful empirical observation, but the novelty is limited (known problem, now quantified), the method contribution is preliminary with severe tradeoffs, and the related work positioning is insufficient for a venue like CoRL. The two-phase training result is the strongest novel element but needs more seeds, more analysis, and better positioning relative to curriculum learning. With a complete experimental campaign (all seeds, OpenVLA, spatial_bowl), stronger related work, and a more honest framing as \"quantitative diagnosis + preliminary solution direction\" rather than implying methodological novelty, this could reach a 6.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5.** I am familiar with the VLA safety literature, SDF-based collision avoidance, safe RL, and evaluation methodology. I have reviewed the full paper drafts, experimental logs, literature map, and project memory. My uncertainty is primarily around the pending experimental results (LoRA baseline attribution, Phase 2 multi-seed) which could shift the significance score by +/- 1 point."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2406ab946d1d5784 (use SendMessage with to: 'a2406ab946d1d5784' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 68207\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 141754</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f420d955-7ae5-4224-93b3-f4bdf2a953a4","uuid":"18e7142c-f382-4b6f-a538-ca139f5a1d35","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:07:13.006Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a robotics/ML conference review (CoRL). Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Venue Standards\nCoRL (Conference on Robot Learning). Acceptance rate ~36%. OpenReview 1-10 scale:\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept  \n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Not good enough, reject\n- 3: Clear rejection\n\nReview dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\n7-page paper (6 pages content + 1 page references). Only 5 references cited.\n\nThe paper studies VLA model safety evaluation on SafeLIBERO benchmark. Key contributions:\n\n1. **Fake safety taxonomy**: CAR metric is systematically contaminated by action collapse (robot freezes) and geometric path safety (conservative motions coincidentally avoid obstacles). Across Pi0 (170M flow matching), Pi0.5 (300M flow matching), and OpenVLA-7B (autoregressive), 92–99.6% of \"safe\" episodes are fake.\n\n2. **Displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR)**: Corrected metric adding end-effector displacement threshold δ=0.1m.\n\n3. **SDF auxiliary loss**: Signed Distance Field training loss via LoRA on Pi0.5 produces real collision avoidance (+25pp filtered CAR), but TSR drops 73%→7%. 4-point λ curve (λ∈{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1}).\n\n4. **Two-phase training**: SDF pre-training (10K steps) + standard fine-tuning (20K steps) recovers TSR to 48% while retaining 81% safety (CAR 45%).\n\n**Method details**:\n- dfCAR = |{i : d_obs_i < ε ∧ d_ee_i > δ}| / N\n- L_SDF = (1/T) Σ max(0, τ - φ(p_t))² (hinge loss on SDF distance)\n- L = L_action + λ · L_SDF\n- Applied through LoRA adapters on Pi0.5\n\n**References (only 5)**:\n[1] SafeLIBERO Benchmark, 2025\n[2] Physical Intelligence. Pi0, 2024\n[3] Kim et al. OpenVLA, ICML 2024\n[4] Ratliff et al. CHOMP, ICRA 2009\n[5] Hu et al. LoRA, ICLR 2022\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the \"fake safety\" observation? Has this been noted before in robotics or RL safety?\n- Is dfCAR a genuine methodological contribution or a straightforward threshold filter?\n- How novel is applying SDF loss to VLA training? SDF is well-established in motion planning (CHOMP etc.)\n- Is the two-phase training strategy novel? Connections to curriculum learning, progressive training, etc.?\n- Overall: Is this a \"discovery\" paper or a \"known issue, now quantified\" paper?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n- Only 5 references — is this adequate for a venue like CoRL?\n- Missing related work areas:\n  * Safe RL / constrained RL literature\n  * Robot safety metrics beyond CAR\n  * Other VLA safety work\n  * SDF in robotics (beyond just CHOMP)\n  * Metric gaming / Goodhart's Law in ML evaluation\n  * Other manipulation benchmarks with safety metrics\n  * Progressive/curriculum training for safety\n- Does the paper position itself correctly relative to the state of the art?\n- Is the contribution properly scoped relative to existing knowledge?\n\n### Broader Positioning\n- Is this primarily a \"benchmark critique\" paper, a \"method\" paper, or a \"diagnostic + partial solution\" paper?\n- How does the paper's scope match CoRL expectations?\n- Would the findings generalize beyond SafeLIBERO?\n- Is the paper timely and relevant?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide your review in this structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted)\n\n### Missing Related Work (specific papers/areas)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Scoring\n- Originality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a CoRL-level review.\n","agentId":"a2406ab946d1d5784","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","t